<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.claude.browser-spin-guard — the CPU twin of com.claude.compressor-sentinel.

  WHAT IT EXISTS FOR (2026-08-17). An `agent-browser`-owned headless Chrome wedged with every one of
  its service processes — GPU, NetworkService, StorageService, AudioService, VideoCaptureService and
  three renderers — spinning at 86-103% CPU simultaneously: 761% of this box's 1000%, sustained for a
  day and a half. Measured effect: load average 244, 85.9% SYS, 0.0% idle, seconds-per-keystroke lag.
  Killing the tree returned the box to 309% total CPU and load 56 inside one minute.

  WHY NOTHING ALREADY OWNED IT. `cc-reaper garbage` snapshots `pid=,ppid=,etime=,ucomm=` — it has no
  CPU column at all, so a process that is alive and burning a core is invisible to it by
  construction; it collects the residue of DEAD sessions, and this was emphatically not dead.
  `compressor-sentinel` samples pcpu but adjudicates memory, and it was right to stay quiet: segment
  utilisation at the time was 12.8% of limit. The box was not dying of memory. No rung watched CPU.

  StartInterval 300, NOT KeepAlive — AND THIS IS THE OPPOSITE CHOICE FROM THE SENTINEL, DELIBERATELY.
  The sentinel is an internal-loop daemon because every one of its trip terms is a RATE, which needs a
  baseline surviving between reads. This guard's instrument is `ps -o pcpu`, which on Darwin is CPU
  time over the process's whole LIFETIME — a self-contained reading that already encodes "has been
  pegged for a long time" and needs no previous sample to interpret. So a periodic job is not a
  degraded daemon here, it is the correct shape, and it costs one `ps` + one `awk` every five
  minutes instead of a resident process. Against a wedge that ran 1.5 days, a 5-minute detection
  latency is not the binding constraint.

  NO ProcessType, NO Nice, NO LowPriorityIO — for the reason the sentinel's header sets out at
  length, and it binds harder here. This job's whole trigger condition IS a pegged box: measured
  during the incident, `top` read 0.0% idle. A Background-band job is deferred aggressively under
  exactly that load — com.claude.capacity-alarm was measured going 6h24m without emitting a sample
  after a panic reboot at load 140. A guard parked in the band its own trigger condition starves is
  inert precisely when it is needed. Standard band, same as an interactive process.

  IT DETECTS AND SURFACES; IT DOES NOT REAP. `--reap` exists and is tested (tests/browser-spin-guard.bats
  test 9) but is NOT passed below. Acting on live processes is an operator decision in this repo —
  the sentinel's actuator required an explicit in-session authorization. The asymmetry that justifies
  a different default here: the compressor panic gives ~7.6 s of warning, so only an armed actuator
  can act inside it, whereas this wedge ran for a day and a half. A surfaced alarm has abundant time
  to be acted on, and the downside of auto-killing (a browser torn out mid-automation) is real. To
  arm it, add `--reap` to the argument below — that is the whole change.

  --notify is DAMPED IN THE SCRIPT, not here: it speaks on the edge (clean -> spin) and re-asserts
  every 6 h while the condition persists. A 5-minute job that paged every tick for a day and a half
  would teach the operator that the line means nothing, which is the failure mode this repo has
  already paid for in alarm-polarity.

  PATH must carry /usr/sbin and /bin explicitly: the wrapper OVERWRITES launchd's default PATH, and
  `ps`/`date` live in /bin. Omitting them is how a sibling job silently lost three rungs in every
  scheduled run while passing every hand-run from an interactive shell.

  AND IT MUST CARRY THE FNM *ALIAS* PREFIX, NOT THE ONE `command -v` REPORTS. The land gate's
  unattended-path-lint blocked the first version of this file for exactly this, and it was right:
  in an interactive shell `agent-browser` resolves to
  ~/Library/Caches/fnm_multishells/<PID>_<EPOCH>/bin/agent-browser — a PER-SHELL, EPHEMERAL
  directory whose name contains the spawning shell's pid. That path does not exist for a launchd
  job and is different for every shell, so copying what `command -v` prints would have produced a
  PATH that works on the desk and never under launchd. The durable prefix is the fnm default
  alias, ~/Library/Application Support/fnm/aliases/default/bin (verified: agent-browser 0.27.1
  executes from there), which is the same prefix the fleet's ms-365-mcp-server processes run from.

  WHY A MISSING agent-browser WOULD HAVE BEEN SILENT. The script guards the graceful close with
  `command -v agent-browser >/dev/null 2>&1 &&`, so an unreachable binary is not an error — it is a
  SKIPPED teardown. The reap would then go straight to SIGKILL, losing the vendor's own session
  cleanup, and nothing would say so. That is latent today because the job does not pass --reap, but
  this header tells the operator to arm it by adding one word, so the trap was armed for whoever
  did that. A guarded dependency that cannot be reached is the quietest kind of broken.

  Kill switches: CC_SPIN_GUARD=0 in the wrapper, or
  `launchctl bootout gui/$UID/com.claude.browser-spin-guard`.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.claude.browser-spin-guard</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>export PATH="$HOME/.claude/bin:$HOME/Library/Application Support/fnm/aliases/default/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"; exec "$HOME/.claude/scripts/browser-spin-guard.sh" --notify</string>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>300</integer>

    <!-- NO ProcessType. NO Nice. NO LowPriorityIO. See the header — this job's trigger condition is
         a pegged box, and the background band is starved by exactly that. -->

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>  <string>/tmp/claude-browser-spin-guard.stdout.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/claude-browser-spin-guard.stderr.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
